<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khải Định</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Khai Dinh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khải Định was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of the Nguyen dynasty from 1916 to 1925, under the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Placed on the throne after the exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duy-tan">Duy Tân</a>, he cooperated closely with the colonial authorities and visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1922, drawing criticism from nationalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-chau-trinh">Phan Châu Trinh</a>. His elaborate tomb near Huế combines Vietnamese and European styles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khai-dinh.jpg" alt="Khải Định" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. Manuel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kh%E1%BA%A3i_%C4%90%E1%BB%8Bnh,_empereur_d%27Annam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khải Định was born Nguyễn Phúc Bửu Đảo on 8 October 1885, the son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-khanh">Đồng Khánh</a>, but he did not succeed his father immediately. Before him the French had exiled two emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-thai">Thành Thái</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duy-tan">Duy Tân</a>, for their resistance to the colonial regime. They then chose Bửu Đảo because his father had been the most submissive of the Nguyễn collaborators with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and had opposed any independence movement. He married his first wife, Trương Như Thị Tịnh, in 1907, when he was still Duke of Phụng Hóa. She left him in 1915 and became a nun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bửu Đảo became the nominal ruler of Annam on 18 May 1916, after the exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duy-tan">Duy Tân</a>, and took the reign name Khải Định. He said he wanted to restore the prestige of the empire, but his close collaboration with the French made that impossible, and he has been described as a figurehead who followed colonial instructions to give legitimacy to French policies. His consort Hồ Thị Chỉ was a daughter of Hồ Đắc Trung, who became Annam's Minister for Public Instruction. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Nguyễn Phúc Vĩnh Thụy</a>, the later Emperor Bảo Đại, had been born in 1913 to the concubine Hoàng Thị Cúc.</p><p>Khải Định was unpopular. The nationalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-chau-trinh">Phan Châu Trinh</a> accused him of selling out his country while he lived in imperial luxury and the people were exploited. Nguyễn Ái Quốc, later known as Hồ Chí Minh, wrote a play called The Bamboo Dragon that ridiculed him as all ceremony and no power. Nationalist leaders also mocked his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1922 to see the Marseilles Colonial Exhibition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His unpopularity reached its peak in 1923, when he authorised the French to raise taxes on Vietnamese peasants, partly to pay for his palatial tomb, which caused great hardship. He also signed orders for the arrest of nationalist leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau">Phan Bội Châu</a>, who were forced into exile, and followers who were captured were beheaded. Khải Định had poor health, like his father, and died of tuberculosis in the Imperial City of Huế on 6 November 1925, at the age of forty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khải Định” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Khai Dinh</b> (r. 1916–1925) cooperated closely with the French, and Bao Dai, emperor from 1926, attempted modest reforms after returning from education in France in 1932 but had little power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Huế on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7480 1552 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Khải Định"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
